let's talk about social media today and  this debate we've talked about that's  been taking place about whether big  social media companies like Twitter and  YouTube and Facebook should be regulated  by the government to prevent what some  are calling censorship we're probably  all familiar with this phenomenon of  social media companies temporarily or  sometimes permanently banning creators  on their platforms for violating Terms  of Service and especially after the  recent ordeal with Alex Jones and the  backlash that followed there has been a  lot more focus on this issue and in fact  there was sort of a national discussion  which is still taking place about number  one should First Amendment free speech  privileges extend to major social media  platforms owned by private companies if  so at what point are the companies big  enough the platform's big enough where  that should be the case how much power  should these platforms have 2d platform  individuals like Alex Jones does the  government have a responsibility to step  in and prevent what some are considering  dangerous censorship and the Alex Jones  incident specifically prompted the  Justice Department to actually look into  this and to start an investigation into  supposed censorship and political bias  by these social media companies social  media executives have been questioned  that Senate hearings about this and  there's a certain contingent of the  public that is sort of in favor of  government intervention to enforce free  speech on major social media platforms  whatever that means and we'll talk about  that even President Trump weighed in  where he said quote social media is  totally discriminating against  Republican conservative voices speaking  loudly and clearly for the Trump  administration we won't let that happen  they are closing down the opinions of  many people on the right while at the  same time doing nothing to others  censorship is a very dangerous thing and  absolutely impossible to police if you  were weeding out fake news there is  nothing so fake as CNN and MSNBC and yet  I do not ask that their sick behavior be  removed social media platforms should be  audited and regulated when it comes to  preventing foreign influence and  American political elections and affairs  particularly because much of it would  just simply entail better enforcement of  already existing laws that are designed  to protect the democratic processes in  the United States so there's actually no  reason for any controversy there  although it's not clear that the Trump  administration is actually really  concerned about that at least not that  we've seen but as I've said in previous  segments I am not in favor of having the  government force privately-owned social  media companies to publish the content  of people that they don't want to be  associated with even if it's supposedly  in the name of free speech  it's a knee-jerk reaction to a problem  that I don't even really think exists no  one can honestly say that these  platforms like YouTube for example  aren't accommodating right-wing voices  that's one of the most ridiculous  aspects of all of this which is how  fabricated all of this social media free  speech outrages both left and right wing  channels have been affected by YouTube D  monetization including our Channel  some have tried to argue that YouTube is  overrun or completely dominated by  right-wing channels I don't agree with  all of those claims because progressives  do have a strong presence but the right  is also not having trouble on YouTube  and you might even be able to say that  YouTube is the home of the Millennial  conservative movement almost all of the  right-wing or anti PC personalities who  are popular among Millennials oh their  fame and great part to YouTube steven  crowder more than two and a half million  subscribers  Jordan Peterson million and a half  subscribers The Daily wire has about a  million rebel media Rubin report  Mylo you na police has a strong presence  stefan molyneux lauren southern Blair  White Gavin McInnes even out white all  right white nationalists like Jared  Taylor and other  do really well on YouTube and you might  say well hold on a second  some of those people have been banned or  they've been suspended on Twitter and I  think that that was unfair but the thing  is that plenty of people further to the  left or in the middle or with no  political affiliation get banned all the  time and often for no good reason and  Twitter has also received a ton of  criticism for not being vigilant enough  about neo-nazis another right-wing  extremists on the platform as has  YouTube so I think a lot of these deep  platforming processes are just  inefficient or admittedly too opaque but  there is simply not enough evidence that  the most popular social media platforms  are making it difficult for  conservatives to broadcast their message  just on the basis of their being  conservative so now that we've  identified what the problem is and is  not what is the solution number one the  inconsistency and non transparency with  regard to the D platforming by social  media companies is the real problem it's  not the fact that they're able to choose  whose content not to publish I have no  problem with that because that is their  right who they block how they do it and  why that's up to them instead of trying  to solve this false dichotomy of who  should control speech Silicon Valley or  the government we should be asking  ourselves how can we preserve private  companies First Amendment rights but  shift the decision-making power back to  the hands of the people and the answer  is for social media companies to  establish platform policies that are  more clearly defined and more  even-handedly enforced vague and briefly  described rules about hate speech or  defamation or offensive content or  whatever leave the creator not knowing  where the line is and it leaves the  public not knowing whether the company  was being impartial when making a  decision to de platform somebody like  Alex Jones and maybe some sort of public  statement about why someone was banned  and a description of the process should  be offered  if the D platformed person had more than  a certain amount of engagement or  followers on the platform we don't get  anything close to that  most of the time with the majority of  these platforms and it would renew some  of that lost trust or at least create  some transparency where the public is  able to judge for themselves is the  platform banning people in a way that is  consistent with its own guidelines and  how are they applying those guidelines  in practice  why would social media companies do this  you might be asking that's a good  question how do we actually make it  happen we have to demand it of them  right undoubtedly these companies will  have to become more transparent and  accountable about algorithms and  flagging and suspending accounts if it  is something that will affect their  image and their brand in a way that has  an impact right failing to do so if it  makes them less competitive against  other social media platforms will force  them inevitably to come along and offer  a solution to the problem that we've  identified here the first battle for the  social media market was about  functionality and addictiveness and  services like Facebook and Instagram and  Twitter and YouTube they won that battle  right but many have said that as we  become more pessimistic about social  media companies what they do with our  data how they handle things like the  platforming that finding solutions to  those types of issues will actually  become the next battle for gaining and  retaining users and that there will  ultimately be some winners and losers we  think of something like Facebook as this  sort of eternal monolith but they've  been having some pretty serious PR  issues over the last couple of years  especially the last six months and that  comes with real financial consequences  for the company  I don't think individual social media  platforms are as immortal as some make  them out to be remember what happened to  MySpace look at the downfall of tumblr  for example although it's still around  Twitter and Facebook themselves have  been having issues in terms of user  numbers especially lately and it's been  affecting their stock prices as well  social media is in its infancy and we  can be certain that Facebook Twitter and  YouTube are not the indefinite be-all  end  all of online communities more are going  to come and go and here's the ultimate  irony with all of this about regulating  social media censorship and I've talked  about this before and you knew I was  going to talk about it today it's  usually the right that's determined to  preserve the free expression of private  corporations and when it comes to  anything else like Environmental  Protection consumer protection workers  rights stabilizing the economy it's the  right who always says the free market is  going to work everything out there's a  natural incentive there it doesn't  matter how big or powerful the companies  become there will be free market  pressures that will force them to  ultimately do the right thing we don't  need to worry about it we don't need to  regulate it our right wingers now  arguing that Oh certain energy companies  have become so dominant that we have to  ensure that they adhere to their  responsibilities to the public to  mitigate environmental harm the way they  are saying Facebook has a responsibility  to the public is nothing they are  willing to say when it comes to energy  companies financial companies or any  others it's only when the right  perceives that their group their side  their tribe is getting the short end of  the stick do they all of a sudden want  to impinge on that free market and they  choose to be outraged it's sort of like  when right-wing commenters or  right-wingers on the internet talk about  how there's this big conspiracy in  Hollywood to promote a progressive  agenda to be totally realistic the  corporations that control Hollywood  don't care about anything but making  money and Hollywood makes money by  taking the temperature of the country  and giving consumers what they want  whether it's TV or movies or music or  video games these occupy people's  attention as much as social media and  it's about making money  ultimately are we also going to regulate  the messages of those industries the way  right-wingers are saying we should  regulate social media I'm not hearing  that what about the advertising industry  like Nike and Colin Kaepernick it's only  about making calculated financial this  visions to improve the company's bottom  line it's not about political propaganda  for its own sake and it's the same thing  with social media companies Facebook and  YouTube didn't ban Alex Jones to push a  progressive political agenda  they banned Alex Jones because their  brands were suffering when they were  seen as mouthpieces for an unhinged  conspiracy theorist one who they also  considered by the way to have violated  their Terms of Service which has granted  something they could have done a better  job about spelling out like I said but  Facebook and Twitter are not responsible  for the free speech of others you are  responsible for finding a platform on  which to speak you are responsible for  finding content you want to consume and  the Internet makes it really easy  there are even a lot of new apps and  websites that let you consolidate all  your social media feeds into one and  those types of services are making this  free speech controversy even more moot  and we really shouldn't forget in the  end that mainstream corporate media  companies like Fox News who are really  pushing this whole social media free  speech regulation agenda are doing so  out of their own interests as well which  is something that's often not considered  in the debate it's sort of like when the  Wall Street Journal and CNN write  articles about YouTube and advertisers  because any new regulation or roadblocks  for new media are a win for them and  government regulation of free speech may  not be as neutral as many think that it  would be you would have enforcement that  could change with administrations and  congressional majorities one proposal  has been to form a commission similar to  the FCC where officers are appointed by  the President and approved by the Senate  and conservatives should be just as  afraid of that idea as progressives  because the entire thing would be a big  mess subject to the whims of whoever's  in the White House and controls the  Senate the entire internet itself as a  whole is a shared resource and a public  square which yes should be kept free and  open and equitably available to the  public with the help of government but  not the  privately owned platforms that operate  within it there isn't enough  justification to encroach on the rights  of those businesses to make their own  decisions on behalf of their  shareholders there are definitely  problems right now with how some of  these social media platforms handle  reprimands and consequences but the  government coming in and imposing harsh  control of tech companies to enforce  this sort of amorphous unspecified  version of political neutrality would be  very sloppy it would be hasty and it  would be an absolutist response and  importantly it would be one that is  ironic when coming from mostly small  government conservatives as they  describe themselves the current  situation as imperfect as it may be is  actually more tenable than what many are  proposing in terms of broad aggressive  anti censorship enforcement and I think  that a free-market alternative should be  our first line of the fence and after  all isn't that what conservatives have  been calling for all along in every  other industry

